Barokeraunia

Ingredients: OLD SKOOL RAP PIONEERS + PROGRESSIVE, ART- & SYMPHONIC ROCK + CYBERPUNK & DYSTOPIAN + Marimba + 1960s ERA
Creative Catalyst: The genre honors a lost radio station that only broadcast during thunderstorms.

Barokeraunia sounds like an ornate 1979 block-party jam hijacked by a 1967 conservatory chamber ensemble and wired into a rain-lashed high-voltage transformer: rapid-fire, boastful old-skool master-of-ceremonies rhymes trade bars with dizzying, polyrhythmic marimba arpeggios that have been thrust through howling germanium fuzz boxes, all cresting over colossal Mellotron orchestral suites and the raw crackle of ionized air.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: Metric modulation swinging unpredictably between a strutting 96–102 BPM boom-bap bounce and labyrinthine 11/8 or 7/4 symphonic gallops that abruptly collapse back into heavy, head-nodding 1960s breakbeats.
- Harmonic Palette: Aeolian and Dorian modes colliding violently with baroque Picardy thirds, whole-tone marimba runs, and chromatic tape-saturated organ suspensions steeped in mid-sixties psychedelic gloom.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Convective Lightning-Gating: Master compression sidechains wired to room-mic bursts of natural thunderclaps, causing the entire mix to violently dip and swell with incoming atmospheric electricity.
  2. Fuzzed Rosewood Percussion: Five-octave concert marimbas captured via contact transducers shoved into vintage 1966 germanium treble boosters, generating warm, percussive wood tones that disintegrate into spitting harmonic distortion.
  3. Galvanic Flow-Stacking: Vocalists triple-track their rhythmic, old-skool park-jam cadence routines in tight fourths and fifths, tracking through carbon-capsule ribbon microphones heavily saturated by vacuum-tube radio transmitters.
- Dynamics: Extreme architectural swings; long, twenty-minute modular compositions where sweeping, majestic Mellotron flute and choir passages dwindle to lone, dry marimba strikes before exploding without warning into thunderous, full-throated rap choruses backed by overdriven timpani.

Scene & Origin Lore
Barokeraunia hatched along the copper-sheathed tenements and flood-prone transformer towers of the Lower Overtown Basin during the late-century brownouts, sparked by the myth of Radio Cumulus-9—a ghost pirate station that went live only when severe cumulonimbus cells breached the city’s upper troposphere. Subculture devotees called "Storm-Phaetons" prowl crumbling tar rooftops clad in sodden 1960s mod velvet coats lined with Faraday copper meshing, carrying lead-weighted marimba mallets and vintage magnetic reel-to-reels in waterproofed military satchels. Whenever weather radar shows squall lines blooming red, the Phaetons rig aerial wires to rusted lightning rods, clustering in rooftop cupolas to hold frantic "spark-jams." They trade elaborate twenty-four-bar rhyming boasts against the lightning flashes, syncing complex marimba counterpoint to the distant rolling thunder in an ecstatic communal trance to channel the drowned voice of the sky-station.

Typical Instruments: Five-octave rosewood concert marimba (run through 1960s Maestro Fuzz-Tone pedals and Watkins Copicat tape delays), Mellotron M400 (flute, cello, and 8-voice choir tape racks), Farfisa Compact Duo organ, 1965 Ludwig drum kit with calfskin heads, custom Shure 55 microphones with copper lightning-grounding leads, and raw piezo contact pickups clamped directly to iron lightning rods.

